  "query_preview": "..\n\nCool start to the morning as temperatures continue to fall with an \nexpected landing in the low to mid 20s by sunrise. A broad Midwest \nhigh pressure will build over the Great Lakes today into Wednesday \nhelping clear out the lake clouds to start the day. A cold airmass \nwill hold through today with 850mb temperatures to around -5C this \nafternoon as the thermal trough gradually draws east. This airmass \nwill support well below normal high temperatures today with most \nlocations topping out in the upper 30s during the afternoon. \nIncreasing coverage of clouds will develop this evening as a \nCanadian wave drops out of southern Alberta and through the Midwest \ninto Wednesday while interacting and eventually phasing with a \nshortwave moving across the southern gulf states. Some moderation in \nthe local airmass will occur Wednesday as warmer air is drawn in \nunder an east-southeast flow as 850mb temperatures climb to above 0C.\nThis should help temperatures for Wednesday reach back into the 40s \nwhile influence of departing high helps keep conditions dry for one \nmore day. \n\nAttention turns to the phasing of the two waves turns into a strong, \nclosed upper level trough with a surface low forming off the Mid-\nAtlantic coast and traveling northeast into the New England region \nduring the late week period. EPS MSLP Member Low spread now points \ntoward a secondary low pressure developing within the broader \nsurface pressure center that will lift out of the Ohio Valley into \nthe eastern Great Lakes. Strong low level jet winds around this low \nwill drive strong moist advection into the central Great Lakes and \nlead to likely precipitation chances for Thanksgiving day. \nPrecipitation looks like it will start to lift across the southern \nMI border Thanksgiving morning and continue to spread northward with\nlikely widespread rainfall at least by Thanksgiving evening if not \nearlier. Moisture wrapping around the system will allow precipitation\nto continue into Friday as flow turns out of the north-northwest. \nTotal QPF amounts may end up with a decent gradient from the Saginaw\nBay to the Detroit Metro region with the higher QPF potential to 1 \ninch or greater focused across Detroit Metro region and other \nsoutheast portions of the CWA. In terms of p-type, a mild set up for \nThursday with a high DGZ will lead to rain as predominate p-type \nuntil we move into Thursday night where lower level thermal profiles \ncool enough to see some windows of wet snow flakes to mix. Warming \nsurface lower on Friday will again become mostly rain before \nprecipitation eventually come to an end towards Friday evening as \nprecipitation shield pulls east with the low pressure system. \n\nTemperatures holding around seasonal norms into and over the weekend \nwith any break in precipitation possibly being brief as troughing \nagain targets the Great Lakes. Timing uncertainty exists, but \nchances for precipitation in the form of mainly rain and perhaps \noccasional rain/snow mix will be possible over the weekend into \nearly next week.",
